### Who was Vladimir Komarov and what was his role in the Soviet space program?
Answer: Vladimir Komarov was a Soviet test pilot, aerospace engineer, and cosmonaut who played a pioneering role in the Soviet space program, most notably as the commander of Voskhod 1—the first spaceflight to carry a multi-person crew—and as the solo pilot of Soyuz 1, making him the first Soviet cosmonaut to fly in space twice; his career also included significant contributions to spacecraft design, cosmonaut training, and public relations before his death during the Soyuz 1 mission in 1967.

Source URL: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vladimir_Komarov

### On what date and mission did Vladimir Komarov die?
Answer: Vladimir Komarov died on April 24, 1967, during the Soyuz 1 mission. The source states he was "the first human to die during a space mission, on Soyuz 1" and notes the "Decay Date: 1967-04-24."

Source URL: https://strela-watch.de/vladimir-komarov/

### What were the technical failures or circumstances that caused the death of Vladimir Komarov upon re-entry?
Answer: Vladimir Komarov's death was caused by a combination of technical failures during the Soyuz 1 mission. The primary issues began when one solar panel failed to deploy, severely reducing electrical power and compromising orientation systems, forcing an early re-entry. Upon re-entry, although the drag chute deployed, a flaw in the parachute compartment housing—its interior was too rough—prevented the main parachute from fully deploying. Komarov then released the reserve chute, but it became entangled with the drag chute, leading to a fatal crash into the ground near Orenburg.

Source URL: https://strela-watch.de/vladimir-komarov/

### Where did the spacecraft carrying Vladimir Komarov crash or land after re-entry?
Answer: The spacecraft carrying Vladimir Komarov crashed into a field near Orenburg at 03:24 GMT after re-entry.

